=========================================
C++17 if constexpr
INTERVIEW NOTES
=========================================

1. What is if constexpr?

if constexpr is a C++17 feature that provides compile-time conditional branching.

The condition is evaluated during compilation.
Only the selected branch is compiled, and the other branch is discarded.

Syntax:

if constexpr(condition)
{
    // true branch
}
else
{
    // false branch
}


2. Why was it introduced?

Before C++17, type-based branching in templates required:
- SFINAE
- std::enable_if
- Template specialization

if constexpr provides a simpler and cleaner way.

std::is_pointer → introduced in C++11 (type trait)
std::is_pointer_v → introduced in C++17 (variable template shortcut)

std::is_pointer_v was introduced in C++17.
It is a variable template version of std::is_pointer.

Before C++17:
std::is_pointer<T>::value #include <type_traits>


3. Simple Example

#include <iostream>
#include <type_traits>
using namespace std;

template<typename T>
void print(T value)
{
    if constexpr(is_pointer_v<T>)
        cout << *value;
    else
        cout << value;
}

int main()
{
    int x = 10;

    print(x);
    print(&x);
}


Output:

10
10


4. Why normal if does not work?

Example:

template<typename T>
void print(T value)
{
    if(is_pointer_v<T>)
        cout << *value;
    else
        cout << value;
}


print(10);

Problem:

Even though condition is false, compiler still checks both branches.

*x is invalid for int, so compilation fails.


With if constexpr:

if constexpr(is_pointer_v<T>)

Compiler removes invalid branch before compilation.


5. Difference between if and if constexpr

Normal if:
- Runtime decision
- Both branches must be valid
- Condition evaluated at runtime


if constexpr:
- Compile-time decision
- Only selected branch is compiled
- Invalid branch is discarded
- No runtime overhead


6. Common Type Traits

is_integral_v<T>

is_pointer_v<T>

is_same_v<T,int>

is_floating_point_v<T>


7. Interview Answer (30 seconds)

"if constexpr was introduced in C++17 for compile-time conditional branching.
Unlike normal if, the compiler evaluates the condition during compilation and
discards the unused branch. It is mainly useful in template programming where
different code needs to be generated based on the type. It simplifies code that
previously required SFINAE or enable_if."


